# mgsilvr.msg - Silver, outside the wall (mgsilvr.ssl). One entry per line; no braces in comments.

# 100 to 102: look (stranger, known) and examine.
{100}{}{You see a thin woman watching you from under a lean-to.}
{101}{}{You see Silver.}
{102}{}{She has the look of somebody who left in a hurry and has been arriving nowhere ever since. One hand never strays far from a knife.}

# 110 to 112: floats. 120, 121: when she is the player's enemy. 125: caught stealing.
{110}{}{Keep walking.}
{111}{}{Nothing here worth the trouble.}
{112}{}{Wall's that way. So's everything else.}
{120}{}{Stay back!}
{121}{}{I knew he'd send somebody!}
{125}{}{Hands off! I've little enough!}

# 150 to 152: message window.
{150}{}{Silver hands you three hundred dollars.}
{151}{}{Silver turns out her pockets and hands you all the money she has. It is less than three hundred.}
{152}{}{Silver counts a hundred dollars into your hand.}

# 200 to 205: greetings.
{200}{}{That's close enough. If you're selling, I'm broke. If you're buying, I'm retired. And if Moriarty sent you, I was never here.}
{201}{}{You again. Still broke. Still retired.}
{202}{}{You've got his money. What's left, my shoes? Go away.}
{203}{}{We had a deal. I'm gone, remember? Ghosts don't chat.}
{204}{}{You did me a kindness. I don't get many. Don't stand there making it strange.}
{205}{}{Moriarty's dead? [She laughs until it turns into a cough.] Then I owe nobody anything, and it only cost me two years.}

# 210 to 216: hub options (214 to 216: low Intelligence).
{210}{}{Moriarty wants his three hundred.}
{211}{}{I've changed my mind. I want the money.}
{212}{}{Who are you?}
{213}{}{I'm going.}
{214}{}{You live in dirt house?}
{215}{}{Yes. Dirt house. Mine. Go and find your own.}
{216}{}{Okay.}

# 220 to 222: who she is.
{220}{}{Silver. I poured drinks at Moriarty's, and did the other thing, until I noticed the debt grew faster than I could work. So I left. Now I have a lean-to, a view of the wall, and debts that are all in his head.}
{221}{}{Why stay so close to town?}
{222}{}{Because it's two days' walk to anywhere, and I have seen what walks those two days. Here there's only the one monster, and he never comes outside.}

# 230 to 237: the demand.
{230}{}{Of course he does. And he sent you, because he hasn't stepped outside that wall in ten years. Three hundred. By my count it's wages. By his I owe him for the air.}
{231}{}{Pay him and he forgets your name. That's cheap, for freedom.}
{232}{}{Hand it over, or I take it. Your choice how.}
{233}{}{Tell me your side of it.}
{234}{}{[Attack] I'll take it off your body.}
{235}{}{I'll come back.}
{236}{}{[A long breath.] ...Free of him, for three hundred. I'd have paid that the first week. Here. Tell him I counted it twice, the way he taught me.}
{237}{}{All right! All right. Here. Take it. You and him should get along. Same animal, different teeth.}

# 240 to 246: her side and her offer.
{240}{}{Two years I worked that bar for a bed and a line in his ledger. The three hundred is what he owed me. Here's a better bargain: a hundred to you, now, and you tell him my camp was cold.}
{241}{}{Deal. You were never here.}
{242}{}{Keep your money. You were never here.}
{243}{}{No. All three hundred.}
{244}{}{I'll think about it.}
{245}{}{Then we're done. He'll look at you a long while when you tell him. Don't blink. He can smell a lie on anybody with less practice than him.}
{246}{}{For nothing? Nobody does a thing for nothing. [She studies you.] ...Huh. Maybe one does. Thank you. Now go, before I start trusting people.}

# 250 to 252: violence, and a broken deal.
{250}{}{Then come and earn it!}
{251}{}{We had a deal! ...Of course. Why would you be any different.}
{252}{}{[Fight.]}
